<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Medici</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1434">1434</a>–<a href="/years/1737">1737</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Medici family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Medici was a Florentine banking family that dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> from 1434, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo the Elder</a> returned from exile. Its members included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo the Magnificent</a> and the popes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Leo X</a> and Clement VII, and from 1532 they ruled as dukes of Florence and from 1569 as grand dukes of Tuscany. The main line died out in 1737, and the family's art collections remained in Florence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-medici.jpg" alt="House of Medici" width="320" height="373" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heralder · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Augmented_Arms_of_Medici.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Medici came from the agricultural Mugello region north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and are first mentioned in a document of 1230. The origin of the name is uncertain; medici is the plural of the Italian word for doctors. Members of the family rose in the early 14th century in the wool trade, though they were far less notable than the Albizzi or the Strozzi. Involvement in a plot in 1400 banned all but two branches of the family from Florentine politics for twenty years.</p><p>In 1397 Giovanni di Bicci de' Medici founded the Medici Bank in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and became one of the city's richest men. He never held political office. The bank, which lasted until 1494, was the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the 15th century, and was among the earliest businesses to use double-entry bookkeeping.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Giovanni's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo the Elder</a> was exiled by the Albizzi in 1433, but in 1434 a pro-Medici signoria was elected and he returned. Cosimo, his son Piero and his grandson Lorenzo dominated Florentine government through most of the 15th century without abolishing it, rarely holding official posts. When Lorenzo died in 1492 his son Piero proved unable to meet the French invasion, and within two years he and his supporters were exiled. The family returned in 1512, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> was in other hands again between 1527 and 1530.</p><p>The senior line ended with the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de' Medici</a>, first Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in 1537; power passed to the junior branch, beginning with Cosimo I. The family held the title Duke of Florence from 1532 and the Grand Duchy of Tuscany from 1569. Two Medici popes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x">Leo X</a> and Clement VII, ruled in the 16th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-de-medici">Catherine de' Medici</a> and Marie de' Medici became queens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>Cosimo II is remembered as patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galileo-galilei">Galileo Galilei</a>, who dedicated his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidereus-nuncius">Sidereus Nuncius</a> of 1610 to him. By 1705 the grand ducal treasury under Cosimo III was nearly bankrupt. The last Medici grand duke, Gian Gastone, died in 1737.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Their chief accomplishments were in sponsoring art and architecture. Giovanni di Bicci aided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaccio">Masaccio</a> and commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a> to rebuild the Basilica of San Lorenzo in 1419. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo the Elder</a>'s circle included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatello">Donatello</a> and Fra Angelico, and Lorenzo invited the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a> to study the family's collection of antique sculpture and was patron to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> for seven years. Contemporaries and later writers disagreed about the family, some calling it corrupt, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>'s The Prince was in the end dedicated to Lorenzo di Piero de' Medici.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Medici” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Grand Duchy grew from the Florentine Republic, which the <b>Medici family</b> had dominated in the fifteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Venice, ruled by elected doges from the early 8th century, closed its Great Council in 1297 to the families then sitting in it, and Florence was governed by its guilds under the Ordinances of Justice of 1293 until the <b>Medici family</b> came to dominate it from 1434.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
